<h2 class="tex-section" id="section:factorization-system"><span data-tag="04KF">9.6</span> Factorization Systems</h2>
<p>
Let $G$ and $H$ be groups, and let $\varphi : G \rightarrow H$ be a group homomorphism. Then the image $\operatorname{im}(\varphi ) = \{  h \in H: ( \exists g \in G) \varphi (g) = h \} $ is a subgroup of $H$. Then $\varphi $ can also be viewed as a group homomorphism from $G$ to $\operatorname{im}( \varphi )$ which, to avoid confusion, we will denote by $\varphi _{L}$. It follows that $\varphi $ factors as a composition 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  G \xrightarrow { \varphi _{L} } \operatorname{im}( \varphi ) \xrightarrow { \varphi _{R} } H, \]
</div>
<p>
 where $\varphi _{R}$ is the inclusion map. This factorization is characterized (up to canonical isomorphism) by the requirement that the homomorphism $\varphi _{L}$ is surjective and the homomorphism $\varphi _{R}$ is injective. This situation fits into a general paradigm which is ubiquitous in mathematics: 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-definition" id="06VS">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06VS">Definition <span data-tag="06VS">9.6.0.1</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a category. A <em>factorization system on $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$</em> is a pair $(S_{L}, S_{R})$, where $S_{L}$ and $S_{R}$ are collections of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ which satisfy the following conditions: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(0)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The collections $S_{L}$ and $S_{R}$ are closed under retracts. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
Every morphism $\varphi : X \rightarrow Z$ factors a composition $X \xrightarrow {\varphi _{L}} Y \xrightarrow { \varphi _ R } Z$, where $\varphi _{L}$ belongs to $S_{L}$ and $\varphi _{R}$ belongs to $S_{R}$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
If $f: A \rightarrow B$ is a morphism belonging to $S_{L}$ and $g: X \rightarrow Y$ is a morphism belonging to $S_{R}$, then every lifting problem 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ A \ar [d]^{f} \ar [r] &amp;  X \ar [d]^{g} \\ B \ar@ {--&gt;}[ur] \ar [r] &amp;  Y } \]
</div>
<p>
 has a unique solution. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="06VT">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06VT">Example <span data-tag="06VT">9.6.0.2</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be the category of groups. Then $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ admits a factorization system $(S_ L, S_ R)$, where $S_{L}$ is the collection of surjective group homomorphisms and $S_{R}$ is the collection of injective group homomorphisms. Moreover, analogous statements hold for other many other categories of algebraic structures (such as modules, rings, Lie algebras, and so forth). </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
In categorical homotopy theory, it is often useful to consider a variant of Definition <a data-tag="06VS" href="/tag/06VS">9.6.0.1</a>: 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-definition" id="06VU">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06VU">Definition <span data-tag="06VU">9.6.0.3</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a category. A <em>weak factorization system</em> on $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a pair $(S_ L, S_ R)$, where $S_ L$ and $S_{R}$ are collections of morphisms satisfying conditions $(0)$ and $(1)$ of Definition <a data-tag="06VS" href="/tag/06VS">9.6.0.1</a>, together with the following weak version of $(2)$: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(2_{-})$</dt>
<dd><p>
If $f: A \rightarrow B$ is a morphism belonging to $S_{L}$ and $g: X \rightarrow Y$ is a morphism belonging to $S_{R}$, then every lifting problem 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ A \ar [d]^{f} \ar [r] &amp;  X \ar [d]^{g} \\ B \ar@ {--&gt;}[ur] \ar [r] &amp;  Y } \]
</div>
<p>
 has a solution (which need not be unique). 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="04NB">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04NB">Example <span data-tag="04NB">9.6.0.4</span>.</a> We have already encountered several examples of weak factorization systems $(S_{L}, S_{R} )$ on the category $\operatorname{Set_{\Delta }}$ of simplicial sets: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
We can take $S_{R}$ to be the collection of Kan fibrations and $S_{L}$ the collection of anodyne morphisms (Proposition <a data-tag="00UV" href="/tag/00UV">3.1.8.1</a>). 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We can take $S_{R}$ to be the collection of inner fibrations and $S_{L}$ the collection of inner anodyne morphisms (Proposition <a data-tag="01BP" href="/tag/01BP">4.1.3.2</a>). 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We can take $S_{R}$ to be the collection of left fibrations and $S_{L}$ the collection of left anodyne morphisms (Proposition <a data-tag="014Z" href="/tag/014Z">4.2.4.7</a>). 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We can take $S_{R}$ to be the collection of right fibrations and $S_{L}$ the collection of right anodyne morphisms (Variant <a data-tag="0150" href="/tag/0150">4.2.4.8</a>). 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We can take $S_{R}$ to be the collection of trivial Kan fibrations and $S_{L}$ the collection of monomorphisms (Exercise <a data-tag="00UY" href="/tag/00UY">3.1.8.11</a>). 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
<p> Beware that none of these are factorization systems (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="06VS" href="/tag/06VS">9.6.0.1</a>). </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-exercise" id="04PH">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04PH">Exercise <span data-tag="04PH">9.6.0.5</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{Set}$ denote the category of sets, let $\mathrm{Surj}$ be the collection of surjective functions, and let $\mathrm{Inj}$ be the collection of injective functions. Show that: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
The pair $(\mathrm{Surj}, \mathrm{Inj} )$ is a factorization system on $\operatorname{Set}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The pair $( \mathrm{Inj}, \mathrm{Surj})$ is a weak factorization system on $\operatorname{Set}$ which is not a factorization system. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
<p> See Exercise <a data-tag="04NS" href="/tag/04NS">9.6.6.22</a>. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
Our goal in this section is to introduce $\infty $-categorical versions of Definitions <a data-tag="06VS" href="/tag/06VS">9.6.0.1</a> and <a data-tag="06VU" href="/tag/06VU">9.6.0.3</a>. We begin in §<a data-tag="04MH" href="/tag/04MH">9.6.4</a> by studying lifting problems in the setting of $\infty $-categories. By definition, a lifting problem in an $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a square diagram 
</p>
<div class="equation" id="06VV">
<span class="equation-label"><a data-tag="06VV" href="/tag/06VV">9.17</a></span>
<div>\begin{equation}  \begin{gathered}\label{equation:lifting-problem-preliminary} \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ A \ar [d]^{f} \ar [r] &amp;  X \ar [d]^{g} \\ B \ar@ {--&gt;}[ur] \ar [r] &amp;  Y } \end{gathered} \end{equation}</div>
</div>
<p>
 in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (omitting the dotted arrow), and a solution is an extension to a $3$-simplex of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (not omitting the dotted arrow); see Definition <a data-tag="04MK" href="/tag/04MK">9.6.4.1</a>. We say that a morphism $f: A \rightarrow B$ of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is <em>weakly left orthogonal</em> to a morphism $g: X \rightarrow Y$ of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ if every lifting problem (<a data-tag="06VV" href="/tag/06VV">9.17</a>) admits a solution (Definition <a data-tag="04MY" href="/tag/04MY">9.6.4.8</a>); in this case we also say that $g$ is <em>weakly right orthogonal</em> to $f$. In §<a data-tag="04NJ" href="/tag/04NJ">9.6.6</a>, we study a stronger version of this condition, which requires that the solution is essentially unique (up to a contractible space of choices). In this case, we say that $f$ is <em>left orthogonal</em> to $g$, or that $g$ is <em>right orthogonal</em> to $f$ (Definition <a data-tag="04NN" href="/tag/04NN">9.6.6.1</a> and Corollary <a data-tag="06WM" href="/tag/06WM">9.6.6.24</a>). 
</p>
<p>
In §<a data-tag="04N9" href="/tag/04N9">9.6.5</a>, we study the $\infty $-categorical version of Definition <a data-tag="06VU" href="/tag/06VU">9.6.0.3</a>. Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. We define a <em>weak factorization system</em> on $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ to be a pair $(S_ L, S_ R)$, where $S_ L$ and $S_ R$ are collections of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ which are closed under retracts, where every element of $S_{L}$ is weakly left orthogonal to every element of $S_{R}$ and every morphism $\varphi : X \rightarrow Z$ of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ can be extended to a diagram 
</p>
<div class="equation" id="06VW">
<span class="equation-label"><a data-tag="06VW" href="/tag/06VW">9.18</a></span>
<div>\begin{equation}  \begin{gathered}\label{equation:factorization-LR} \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ &amp;  Y \ar [dr]^{ \varphi _{R} } &amp;  \\ X \ar [ur]^{ \varphi _{L} } \ar [rr]^{ \varphi } &amp;  &amp;  Z, } \end{gathered} \end{equation}</div>
</div>
<p>
 where $\varphi _{L} \in S_{L}$ and $\varphi _{R} \in S_{R}$. In §<a data-tag="04PC" href="/tag/04PC">9.6.8</a>, we study a stronger version of this condition where each element of $S_{L}$ is left orthogonal to each element of $S_{R}$; in this case, we say that the pair $(S_ L, S_ R)$ is a <em>factorization system</em> on $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (Definition <a data-tag="04PD" href="/tag/04PD">9.6.8.1</a>). In §<a data-tag="04PQ" href="/tag/04PQ">9.6.7</a> we show that, under this additional assumption, the diagram (<a data-tag="06VW" href="/tag/06VW">9.18</a>) is unique up to a contractible space of choices and depends functorially on $\varphi $: see Theorem <a data-tag="04QC" href="/tag/04QC">9.6.7.2</a>. 
</p>
<p>
Our main goal in this section is to show that, under some mild set-theoretic assumptions, we can produce factorization systems in great abundance. Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a presentable $\infty $-category. In §<a data-tag="06X2" href="/tag/06X2">9.6.9</a>, we show that every small collection $W$ of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ generates a factorization system $(S_ L, S_ R)$ where $S_{R}$ is the collection of morphisms which are right orthogonal to each element of $W$, and $S_{L}$ is the collection of morphisms which are generated by $W$ under composition and small colimits (Theorem <a data-tag="04PN" href="/tag/04PN">9.6.9.8</a>). This is a consequence of a parallel statement for weak factorization systems (Theorem <a data-tag="04NG" href="/tag/04NG">9.6.5.12</a>), which we prove in §<a data-tag="04N9" href="/tag/04N9">9.6.5</a>. Our proof will use an $\infty $-categorical version of Quillen's <em>small object argument</em>, which we formulate in §<a data-tag="04M7" href="/tag/04M7">9.6.3</a> (Theorem <a data-tag="04MA" href="/tag/04MA">9.6.3.3</a>) following some formal preliminaries laid out in §<a data-tag="04KZ" href="/tag/04KZ">9.6.1</a> and §<a data-tag="04LG" href="/tag/04LG">9.6.2</a>. 
</p>
